= Introduction

Survey results provided by the U.S. Census Bureau have long provided important estimates on various social, economic, housing and demographic topics across the entire country. While these estimates are often reliable for larger areas, estimates of characteristics from smaller areas are often unreliable due to a smaller available sample size and higher sampling variance. This becomes an issue when examining direct survey estimates from sparsely populated areas at the county and census tract level.

One modeling solution for this was proposed by Fay and Herriot in 1979 @FayHerriot1979. This solution involved the model having the capability to "borrow information" from other areas in the overall domain, taking advantage of the known measurement error from the Bureau's direct survey estimates. This is alongside the ability to optionally include auxiliary covariates that can help model the characteristic being estimated. These auxiliary covariates could come from other survey results, or any other dataset that could be of use. This solution works by having a regression model fit to the direct estimates from the survey. The direct estimates are adjusted to these new fitted values based on the amount of measurement error relative to the overall datas random effects. This allows already precise estimates to stay relatively unchanged, while previously unreliable estimates are brought more in line to the new regression values. This mixed linear model is known as the Fay-Herriot model. 

Many variations on this model have been developed over time, such as those that take a different handling of the random effects. The traditional Fay-Herriot model assumes independence of the area-level random effects, which can be problematic in many small area estimation tasks when spatial autocorrelation of the estimates is present. There have been many models for the spatial area effects used in the past. Some related examples being conditional autoregressive (CAR) models, intrinsic autoregressive (IAR) models and intrinsic conditional autoregressive (ICAR) models as used by Porter et al. @PORTER201427.

This project uses a hierarchical Bayesian approach with an ICAR model for the spatial random effects and includes auxiliary information from the 2020 NASS Crop Data Layer. This is done to see if this approach can improve the 5-year (2016-2020) ACS estimates for housing vacancy rates at the county level in Iowa. For model evaluation, the 2020 decennial census is used as the evaluation reference for housing vacancy rates. 5 models will be examined in this project. Four use Bayesian methodologies and one uses a Frequentist method as a reference to ensure correct implementation of the other models. Of the Bayesian models, one contains only an intercept, one an intercept and the auxiliary covariate, one an intercept and ICAR spatial effects, and one that contains all 3. The Frequentist model only contains an intercept. 

This report first covers the motivating data, necessary processing and analysis. Then it covers the technical methodology used for modeling, followed by a breakdown of the results and a final discussion of limitations and overall conclusions. 